    private val textChunks: List<String> = listOf(
        "[0m[1mRule: Fed. R. Civ. P. 47(a) — Examining Jurors[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 47(a), as reproduced\nin the textbook narrative\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 536-37\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: The court may permit the parties or their attorneys\nto examine prospective jurors, or the court may conduct the\nexamination itself; if the court conducts the examination, it\nmust either permit the parties or their attorneys to make any\nfurther inquiry it considers proper, or itself ask any\nadditional questions the parties request that it considers\nproper\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Who is permitted to question prospective\njurors during voir dire, and what happens if the court chooses\nto conduct that questioning itself?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. The court must choose one of two methods of examination —\n    permitting the parties/attorneys to question jurors\n    directly, or conducting the questioning itself\n    2. If the court examines jurors itself, it must allow\n    further inquiry by the parties/attorneys (or ask their\n    additional proper questions itself)\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Trial judges rely on this rule at the outset of\njury selection to decide the format of voir dire in a given\ncourtroom, and parties invoke the \"further inquiry\" clause to\nensure they still get a chance to probe potential bias even when\nthe judge insists on handling the primary questioning\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The court retains discretion over the format;\nthere is no requirement that attorneys be given direct\nexamination of jurors — the rule only guarantees that if the\ncourt examines jurors itself, some avenue for additional\nquestioning (by the court itself or the parties) must exist\n\n[0m[1mRule: Fed. R. Civ. P. 47(b) — Peremptory Challenges[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 47(b), as reproduced\nin the textbook narrative\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 536-37\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: The court must allow the number of peremptory\nchallenges provided by 28 U.S.C. § 1870\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: How many peremptory challenges is each party\nentitled to in federal civil trials, and where is that number\nset?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The court has no discretion to deny the number\nof peremptory challenges fixed by 28 U.S.C. § 1870 — it \"must\nallow\" them\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This rule is invoked whenever a party seeks to\nexercise a peremptory challenge during jury selection, and it\noperates as a cross-reference directing courts to the specific\nnumerical allotment in the companion statute rather than setting\nthe number itself\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: None stated in the rule itself — the actual limits\nand any flexibility (e.g., treating multiple parties as one) are\nfound in § 1870, not in Rule 47(b)\n\n[0m[1mRule: Fed. R. Civ. P. 47(c) — Excusing a Juror[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 47(c), as reproduced\nin the textbook narrative\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 536-37\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: During trial or deliberation, the court may excuse\na juror for good cause\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: What authority does a court have to remove a\njuror after the jury has already been seated and trial or\ndeliberations have begun?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The court must find \"good cause\" before excusing\na juror during trial or deliberation\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This provision is invoked when a seated juror\nbecomes unable or unfit to continue (for example, illness,\nmisconduct, or a disqualifying revelation that surfaces after\nthe jury is empaneled), allowing the court to remove that juror\nwithout derailing the trial\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: This power is distinct from, and exercised later\nin time than, the for-cause dismissals that occur during voir\ndire under the venire-examination process — Rule 47(c) governs\nremoval after the jury is already seated and the trial is\nunderway, not the initial selection process\n\n[0m[1mRule: 28 U.S.C. § 1870 — Peremptory Challenge Allotment[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Statute, 28 U.S.C. § 1870, as reproduced in the\ntextbook narrative\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 536-37\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: In civil cases, each party is entitled to three\nperemptory challenges, and several defendants or several\nplaintiffs may be considered as a single party for purposes of\nmaking challenges, or the court may allow additional peremptory\nchallenges and permit them to be exercised separately or jointly\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: How many peremptory challenges does each side\nget in a federal civil case, and how are challenges allocated\nwhen there are multiple plaintiffs or multiple defendants?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. Baseline allotment of three peremptory challenges per\n    party in civil cases\n    2. Where there are multiple plaintiffs or multiple\n    defendants, the court has discretion either to treat them as\n    a single party (sharing the three challenges) or to grant\n    additional peremptory challenges to be exercised separately\n    or jointly\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Parties invoke this statute when exercising\nstrikes during jury selection, and courts apply it to resolve\ndisputes over how many strikes a side with multiple co-parties\nshould receive\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The three-challenge baseline is not rigid where\nmultiple parties are on one side — the court may expand the\nnumber of challenges and choose whether they are used separately\n(each co-party strikes independently) or jointly (co-parties\nmust agree on a shared strike)\n\n[0m[1mRule: 28 U.S.C. § 1870 — For-Cause Challenges Determined by the[0m\n[0m[1mCourt[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Statute, 28 U.S.C. § 1870, as reproduced in the\ntextbook narrative\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 536-37\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: All challenges for cause or favor, whether to the\narray or panel or to individual jurors, shall be determined by\nthe court\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Who decides whether a prospective juror should\nbe dismissed for cause?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The determination of any for-cause or favor\nchallenge — whether directed at the entire array/panel or at an\nindividual juror — must be made by the court, not by the parties\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This rule is invoked whenever a party asks the\njudge to dismiss a venire member for cause after voir dire\nquestioning reveals a disqualifying bias or connection to the\ncase; unlike peremptory challenges, the party cannot simply\nexercise this dismissal — the party must persuade the court to\ngrant it\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: None stated — this authority rests exclusively\nwith the court and is not delegable to the parties, in contrast\nto peremptory challenges, which parties exercise on their own\nwithout needing the court's agreement on the merits\n\n[0m[1mRule: Textbook Narrative — Jury Pool/Venire and the Voir Dire[0m\n[0m[1mProcess[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Textbook/casebook narrative preceding the Rule 47 and\n§ 1870 text\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 536-37\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Potential jurors called to the courthouse for a\ngiven case form a panel known as the jury pool or venire, and\ncounsel examine members of that pool (directly or through the\njudge) in a process called voir dire, after which a member may\nbe dismissed for cause if questioning reveals a justification\nfor excusing or disqualifying them\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: What is the overall structure of jury\nselection before trial begins, and what is voir dire?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. A venire (pool) of potential jurors is assembled for the\n    specific case\n    2. The basic contours of the dispute are described to the\n    venire\n    3. Counsel or the judge question the venire (voir dire)\n    4. A member is dismissed for cause only if the questioning\n    reveals a justification for excusing or disqualifying them\n    (for example, personally knowing a party, or having already\n    formed an opinion on the outcome)\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This framework describes the sequence a court and\nthe parties follow at the start of every civil jury trial, and\nit sets up the distinction — elaborated in Rule 47 and § 1870 —\nbetween for-cause dismissals (requiring a stated justification,\ndecided by the court) and peremptory challenges (requiring no\nstated reason)\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: None stated beyond the examples given (personal\nknowledge of a party, pre-formed opinion on the outcome) — these\nare illustrative, not an exhaustive list of for-cause grounds\n\n[0m[1mRule: Textbook Narrative — Nature of Peremptory Challenges[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Textbook/casebook narrative (Notes and Questions\nsection) discussing the traditional purpose of peremptory\nchallenges\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 536-37\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Traditionally, a peremptory challenge allows\ncounsel to have a member of the jury pool dismissed for any\nreason or no reason at all, with no obligation to articulate any\nreason for the strike, resting on the premise that trial lawyers\nshould be able to rely on their instincts to strike persons\ntheir gut tells them will be biased against their clients\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: What distinguishes a peremptory challenge from\na for-cause challenge, and what value does the peremptory\nchallenge serve?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: No requirement at all — this is precisely the\ndefining feature: the challenging party need not state, and the\ncourt does not evaluate, any reason for the strike\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Parties use peremptory challenges during voir\ndire to remove jurors they suspect (based on demeanor,\nbackground, or instinct) may be unfavorable, without having to\nsatisfy the court that a legally cognizable \"cause\" exists — in\nprinciple this protects the value of an impartial jury by\nletting attorneys act on subtle cues that wouldn't meet the\nfor-cause standard\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The textbook flags this \"no reason at all\" premise\nas an open question — it asks whether \"any reason at all\" truly\nmeans any reason, and whether other values (implicitly, concerns\nabout discriminatory use of strikes) are at stake, foreshadowing\nthe limits explored in Edmonson v. Leesville Concrete (which is\nreferenced but not itself part of this assigned excerpt)\n\n[0m[1mVoir Dire[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The process of examining prospective jurors —\neither by the parties'/attorneys' direct questioning or by the\njudge questioning them (including on the parties' behalf) — to\ndetermine their fitness to serve on the jury for a given case\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Voir dire is the mechanism through which both\nfor-cause dismissals and peremptory challenges are actually\nexercised; it is the procedural stage governed by Rule 47(a)\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Voir dire is the examination process itself,\ndistinct from the challenges (for-cause or peremptory) that\nresult from it\n\n[0m[1mJury Pool / Venire[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The set of potential jurors called to the\ncourthouse for a given case\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: This is the starting universe from which the\nactual trial jury is selected through voir dire and challenges\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: The venire is broader than the empaneled jury —\nmost members of the venire will be excused or not chosen, either\nthrough challenges or simply not being called forward\n\n[0m[1mChallenge for Cause[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A request by counsel to the court to dismiss a\nmember of the jury pool because questioning revealed a\njustification for excusing or disqualifying that person from\nservice — for example, personally knowing a party or having\nalready formed an opinion on the outcome\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Under § 1870, all for-cause challenges (to the\narray/panel or to individual jurors) are determined by the\ncourt, not the parties, and there is no limit on the number of\nfor-cause challenges tied to the statute's peremptory-challenge\ncap\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Unlike a peremptory challenge, a for-cause\nchallenge requires the challenging party to articulate a\nspecific justification, and the court — not the party — decides\nwhether that justification is sufficient\n\n[0m[1mPeremptory Challenge[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A challenge that allows a party to dismiss a\nmember of the jury pool without stating cause; in federal civil\ncases, each party is entitled to three under 28 U.S.C. § 1870\n(with adjustments possible when there are multiple plaintiffs or\ndefendants)\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Peremptory challenges are the central topic\nintroduced by this reading and set up the next case, Edmonson v.\nLeesville Concrete, which questions whether \"any reason at all\"\ntruly means any reason and whether other legal values limit\ntheir use\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Unlike for-cause challenges, peremptory\nchallenges require no justification and are not subject to the\ncourt's approval on the merits — the party simply exercises the\nstrike, limited only by the numerical cap set by statute\n"
